---
import Project from '../../../layouts/Project.astro';
import ProjectHeader from '../../../components/ProjectHeader.astro';
import ProjectFacts from '../../../components/ProjectFacts.astro';
import Apparatus from '../../../components/Apparatus.astro';
import HallyuCoverage from '../../../components/figures/HallyuCoverage.astro';
import HallyuSeries from '../../../components/figures/HallyuSeries.astro';
import hallyu from '../../../data/hallyu.json';
import { projects } from '../../../data/projects';

const p = projects.find((x) => x.slug === 'hallyu-indian-press')!;
const S = hallyu.print_series;
const blocks = hallyu.periodisation.blocks;

const scale = [
  { n: hallyu.korea_baseline_2000_2010.toLocaleString('en-US'), unit: 'Korea-related articles, 2000–2010' },
  { n: String(hallyu.hallyu_term_uses_2000_2010), unit: 'use of “Hallyu” in that decade' },
  { n: S.total.toLocaleString('en-US'), unit: 'print articles, 2006–2026' },
  { n: '6', unit: 'archives audited' },
  { n: String(hallyu.pilot.corpus_docs), unit: 'documents in the pilot corpus' },
];

const jsonld = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Dataset',
  name: 'Looking for Hallyu in the Indian Press, 2000–2026: source audit and counts',
  description:
    'Counts and source-coverage metadata for Indian English-language newspaper reporting on Korean popular culture, 2000–2026. Newspaper text is copyrighted and is not redistributed; the dataset publishes counts, coverage windows and derived statistics only.',
  creator: { '@type': 'Person', name: 'Mohd Bilal', affiliation: { '@type': 'CollegeOrUniversity', name: 'The Academy of Korean Studies' } },
  temporalCoverage: '2000/2026',
  spatialCoverage: 'India',
  inLanguage: 'en',
  license: 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/',
  version: 'Source audit v1, 18 August 2026',
  isAccessibleForFree: true,
  variableMeasured: [
    'Korea-related article count', 'Hallyu-vocabulary article count',
    'licensed full-text coverage start year', 'print versus online edition objects',
  ],
};
---
<Project title="Looking for Hallyu in the Indian Press"
  description="A source audit and corpus-development project on Indian English-language newspaper reporting of Korean popular culture, 2000–2026. The national press was nearly silent on Hallyu before 2011, and the visibility curve turns in 2018–19."
  citationTitle="Looking for Hallyu in the Indian Press, 2000–2026: A Source Audit"
  jsonld={jsonld}>
  <div class="wrap">

    <ProjectHeader project={p} scale={scale} />

    <div class="prose lead">
      <p>
        This project is building a corpus of Indian English-language newspaper reporting on Korean popular
        culture, in order to ask how that press framed something it had no vocabulary for. What is finished is
        the stage before the framing analysis: an audit of what the archives actually hold. That audit is
        reported here as a result rather than as preparation, because two of its findings are substantive, and
        because the constraints it documents apply to anyone who tries to build this corpus.
      </p>
    </div>

    <section class="section">
      <h2><span class="mark">Finding I</span>An absence, corroborated from two directions</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          During the decade in which Korean popular culture was spreading through Northeast India, the
          English-language national press was nearly silent about it. Licensed full-text search across the
          Times of India, Hindustan Times and Indian Express finds
          {hallyu.korea_baseline_2000_2010.toLocaleString('en-US')} Korea-related articles in 2000–2010, and
          the words “Hallyu” or “Korean wave” appear exactly {hallyu.hallyu_term_uses_2000_2010} time in the
          whole period: a single incidental wire-story use in 2007. Only 58 articles match the project's
          broader candidate vocabulary before 2011, almost all in film-review contexts.
        </p>
        <p>
          A parallel open-web harvest of The Hindu's daily section indexes for the same decade, enumerated
          through the Internet Archive, recovered {hallyu.pilot.hindu_headline_matches} Korea-related
          headlines and a {hallyu.pilot.corpus_docs}-document pilot corpus, and zero culture-context Hallyu
          headlines. Two independent routes into the same decade return the same absence.
        </p>
        <p>
          The silence is national, not total. Korean popular culture was circulating regionally through these
          years, and the project records that circulation in a lightweight evidence register rather than
          treating it as a corpus, because the material behind it is not comparable with national newspaper
          text and would not survive being counted alongside it. Keeping the two apart is what allows the
          absence in the national press to be read as a fact about the national press.
        </p>
      </div>
      <Apparatus label="Why this is a finding and not a gap">
        <p>
          The sources were searched, the criteria are stated, and the retrieval was validated against licensed
          full text. A documented near-zero under stated criteria is evidence about what the press was doing.
          It is distinct from the 2000–2004 window below, where no licensed source exists at all and the
          correct description is unvalidated.
        </p>
      </Apparatus>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2><span class="mark">Finding II</span>The archives change underneath the curve</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Counts of Korean-culture articles do rise steeply after 2011, and they cannot be read as popularity
          until the thing being counted holds still. Each database begins its full text in a different year,
          between 2005 and 2015, and from 2017 all of them start indexing online-only editions alongside
          print. A count that mixes those objects will rise whether or not any newspaper published more.
        </p>
      </div>
      <HallyuCoverage />
      <Apparatus label="A rule this project holds itself to">
        <p>
          The phrase “Hallyu coverage increased” is not used in this project, and variables are named so that
          Korean-popular-culture counts and Hallyu-vocabulary counts can never be silently conflated. Print
          objects are separated from online objects before any comparison, and counts are reported per
          newspaper per year rather than as a single national curve.
        </p>
      </Apparatus>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2><span class="mark">Finding III</span>Once the denominator is held constant, the curve turns in 2018–19</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Restricting the series to print-edition objects removes the 2017 indexing artefact and leaves a
          curve that can be interpreted. It does not turn where an earlier working hypothesis expected. That
          hypothesis put the break at 2017, on the strength of a global BTS breakout; the evidence makes 2017
          a local trough at {S.rows.find((r) => r.year === 2017)?.total} articles, 0.61 times the previous
          year, with the step change arriving in two stages afterwards.
        </p>
      </div>
      <HallyuSeries />
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The periodisation below is therefore derived from the series rather than assumed, which is the
          methodological point of the whole exercise: a periodisation taken from the history of the
          phenomenon would have put the boundary in the wrong place, and a periodisation taken from the raw
          database counts would have put it in the same wrong place for a different reason.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="tablewrap">
        <table>
          <caption class="small muted">Mean print articles per year, by block. The final partial year is excluded from every rate.</caption>
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Block</th><th scope="col" class="num">Articles per year</th><th scope="col">Character</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {blocks.map((b) => (
              <tr><td class="mono">{b.span}</td><td class="num">{b.per_year}</td><td>{b.character}</td></tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <Apparatus label="Which paper carries the story">
        <p>
          The Hindustan Times share of print coverage swings widely and is worth explaining rather than
          smoothing: 80% in 2006 on very small numbers, down to 13.6% in 2013, and back to 80.3% in 2024. It
          is both the earliest substantial carrier and the dominant one in the saturation period, which is why
          provenance — staff, wire or unknown — is a core variable of the design rather than a nuisance field.
          A wire story is not evidence of a newspaper's framing choices.
        </p>
      </Apparatus>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2><span class="mark">Method</span>How the corpus is being built</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          A three-stage pipeline. <b>Discover</b> enumerates archive discovery pages. <b>Harvest</b> fetches
          candidates cache-first at polite, identified, rate-limited speeds, with every HTTP response cached
          permanently so that re-running a stage never re-touches the network. <b>Build</b> compiles a
          full-text-searchable corpus in SQLite with FTS5, carrying provenance for every document.
        </p>
        <p>
          Vocabulary matching is deliberately two-tier: a broad Korea net for recall, then the candidate
          Hallyu vocabulary for precision, evaluated against licensed full-text counts rather than assumed.
          The vocabulary is version 0.9 and is not frozen: it will be fixed only after a retrieval test
          measures its recall and precision on a stratified sample. No paywall and no robots directive is
          bypassed at any stage; where an archive is bot-gated, the licensed copy is the only route used.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2><span class="mark">Rights</span>What will be published, and what never will</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Newspaper text is copyrighted, and this project will not republish it. That is a constraint on the
          shape of the output rather than on its substance: what makes the corpus useful to another researcher
          is the description of it, not the articles themselves.
        </p>
        <ul class="willpublish">
          <li><b>Counts by newspaper and year,</b> print objects separated from online objects, as a citable table with a deposited version.</li>
          <li><b>The source-coverage audit:</b> database windows, the GDELT floor, and web-archive capture rates by year.</li>
          <li><b>The candidate Hallyu vocabulary</b> (v0.9) and the relevance decision tree with its boundary rulings.</li>
          <li><b>Provenance shares</b> — staff, wire, unknown — per newspaper per period.</li>
          <li><b>No article text, ever,</b> and no derived text long enough to substitute for it.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2><span class="mark">Limits</span>What this project does not claim</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <ul class="limits">
          <li><b>No framing analysis exists yet.</b> Everything here is a count or a statement about source availability. No frame, topic or evaluation coding has been applied to a validated sample.</li>
          <li><b>2000–2004 is unvalidated.</b> No licensed full-text source at the host institution covers it, and web-archive coverage of that window is thinnest.</li>
          <li><b>The pre-2011 absence still awaits recall validation</b> against a historical database before it can be published as a strong claim.</li>
          <li><b>Discovery in the web-archive strand is headline-level,</b> which is a known recall limitation.</li>
          <li><b>One annual figure is treated as suspect:</b> the Times of India count for {S.anomaly.year} falls to {S.anomaly.value} between neighbours of 207 and 128, which is more likely a database gap than an editorial decision.</li>
          <li><b>The final year is partial</b> ({S.partial_note}) and is never used in a rate.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="small muted">
          The full source audit, pipeline description and citation are on the
          <a href="/projects/hallyu-indian-press/data/">data page</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <ProjectFacts
      project={p}
      duration="Source feasibility work from August 2026; pilot corpus and licensed validation completed 18 August 2026; collection phases gated and in progress."
      resources={[
        { href: '/projects/hallyu-indian-press/data/', label: 'Method and data', note: 'sources, pipeline, ethics' },
        { href: '/visualizations/#how-to-read', label: 'How to read the figures' },
      ]}
      licence="Counts, coverage metadata and derived statistics: CC BY 4.0. Pipeline code: MIT. Newspaper text is copyrighted, is held locally under licence, and is not redistributed in any form."
      citation="Bilal, Mohd. Looking for Hallyu in the Indian Press, 2000–2026: A Source Audit. Source audit v1. The Academy of Korean Studies, 2026."
      url="https://mohdbilaldh.github.io/projects/hallyu-indian-press/"
      credits="Research, data, code and design: Mohd Bilal (The Academy of Korean Studies). Licensed database access through Yonsei University. No external funding." />
  </div>
</Project>
<style>
  .lead { margin-bottom: 8px; }
  .limits, .willpublish { max-width: var(--w-measure); }
  .limits li, .willpublish li { margin-bottom: 10px; }
</style>
